Red rule: possible impersonation
Go to Exchange admin centre > Mail flow > Rules > Add a rule > Create a new rule.
Name it "Banner - Red - Impersonation".
Under "Apply this rule if", choose The sender > is external/internal > Outside the organization.
Click + to add a second condition: The message headers > includes any of these words. Enter From as the header name, then add the display names of your senior and finance staff, one per entry.
Under "Do the following", choose Apply a disclaimer to the message > prepend a disclaimer.
Click "Enter text" and paste:
html
<div style="background:#FDE7E9;border-left:6px solid #C00000;padding:10px 12px;margin-bottom:12px;font-family:Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:13px;color:#323130"><b style="color:#C00000">Possible impersonation.</b> The sender uses the name of a senior colleague but this email came from outside the organisation. Verify by phone before acting, and report it if unsure.</div>
Click "Select one" and set the fallback action to Wrap.
Under "Except if", choose The subject or body > includes any of these words, and enter Possible impersonation. so the banner isn't added again on replies.
Click Next, tick "Stop processing more rules", and save.
Yellow rule: risky attachment

Repeat steps 1 to 9 with these changes:

Name: "Banner - Yellow - Attachment".
Second condition (step 4): Any attachment > file extension includes these words, then add html, htm, iso, img, one, svg and any others you want.
Exception text (step 8): Caution: unusual attachment.
HTML (step 6):
html
<div style="background:#FFF4CE;border-left:6px solid #E0A800;padding:10px 12px;margin-bottom:12px;font-family:Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:13px;color:#323130"><b style="color:#8A6100">Caution: unusual attachment.</b> This external email contains a file type often used in phishing. Do not open it unless you were expecting it.</div>

For a yellow banner on payment or password wording, create a third rule the same way with The subject or body > includes any of these words as the second condition, and change the banner text to suit.

Finishing off
Order: in the rules list, move the red rule to the top (priority 0) and yellow below it, with any generic external rule last.
Enable: new rules are created switched off, so turn each one on from the rules list.
Test: send from an outside mailbox with a display name on your list, and another with an .html attachment. Changes can take a little while to apply.

Two limits to expect: a later reply in an already-bannered thread arrives without a fresh banner at the top because of the step 8 exception, and Outlook's dark mode may shift the colours slightly.
